window.docContents[250].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--System-context-card":{"contents":"* Scope* System or component* Sort / status* L0 assumptions and attributes\n* System* The oscillator* Spring–mass oscillator* One body, one linear spring, a wall, and a line of motion.\n* Inside* Body* Homogeneous, rigid, point-like body* One mass; assumptions to be revisited at L3.\n* Inside* Spring* Linear spring* One spring; massless and ideal.\n* Outside* Wall* Fixed, infinitely massive wall* Boundary condition for the spring.\n* Outside* Line* One-dimensional line of motion* The motion is constrained to this line.\n\nThis section records the bearer informally. The Lean expressions are dimension- or kind-indexed quantities without an object index; L1 introduces the bearer into the type.\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L0 — The black box","header":"5.1.10. System-context card","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--System-context-card"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Risks-at-this-step":{"contents":"* Wrong carving. For body A of pair P, counting a half twice or leaving one out changes the whole mass; the pinned decomposition gives one slot to each half and proves the total at HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#opened_body.* Weighted mean, not sum. The centre of mass uses each half's mass and position, while both halves share the same angular velocity; the pinned checks are HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#center_of_mass_mean and HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#angular_velocity_intensive.* Degenerate whole and frame. A massless body's centerOfMass evaluates to the origin because Lean defines 1/0 = 0, which is not a physical centre of mass; and lab-frame angular velocity is not the body-frame input to rotational energy; the checks are HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#massless_center_of_mass, HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#bare_frame_mismatch, and HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens#framed_rotational_energy.\n\nFurther reading\n\n* Exhibits/Composition, findings 2 and 5.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L3 — The body opens up","header":"5.4.4. Risks at this step","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Risks-at-this-step"}});